// Three findings, one shape underneath. Each was filed against a different rule and each
// reads, on its own, like a rough edge:
//
//   ITER.260823.08/F1  the gate re-pinned all five claims as pending, though four had been
//                      met and WITNESSED by ITER.260823.07 minutes earlier
//   ITER.260823.08/F2  an iteration carrying out a decision a prior one sealed passed
//                      `implement-follows-an-approach` by having NO design at all
//   ITER.260823.06/F4  `the-plan-and-the-code-agree` refuses every approach of an iteration
//                      whose design is done and whose implement is not — the normal in-flight
//                      state of anything that did what TS.260821.19 asks
//
// They are one fault. Every completeness question in this engine — which claims are owed,
// which approach was followed, which phase is done — is asked of ONE iteration, as though the
// iteration's span and the slice's life were the same span. They are not. `a-slice-took-more-
// than-one-iteration` is a fitness function this method declares and expects to fire; the
// model it is a fitness function OF cannot represent the thing it measures.
//
// So the record answers "has this been shown?" with "not in this iteration", and an agent
// reading that answer does the only thing available: it re-derives what the record already
// holds. That is S7, produced by the mechanism that exists to end S7.
//
// The cost so far has been cheap and arguably good — four re-runs against a tree that had
// moved. It stops being cheap the first time a claim is settled by something slow, and it is
// already not cheap for F2 and F4, where the two routes past the rule are to under-declare
// the design or to write the plan after the code.

thin-slice "TS.260823.10" {
    slug "an-iteration-is-not-the-first"
    title "Ask what the slice has shown, not what this iteration has shown"
    kind "command"
    realizes "CAP.claim-settlement"
    from-storm "ES.260819.01"

    command "close"
    actor "the maintainer taking a second iteration on a slice the first one did not finish"
    produces-event "ClaimCarriedForward" "ContinuationRefused"
    needs-read-model "what-this-iteration-claims-and-has-shown"

    trigger "a second iteration on a slice re-owes every claim the first one already witnessed"
    outcome "an iteration states what it continues, and every completeness question is answered from the slice's history rather than from one iteration's"

    scenario #"""
        Given a slice whose first iteration settled some of its claims and closed without
             delivering it
        When a second iteration opens on that slice
        Then it names the one it continues, arrives owing the claims that are still open and
             CARRYING the ones already witnessed — with the prior evidence cited, not copied —
             and it can say which iteration's approach it is carrying out. Re-witnessing a
             carried claim stays available and stays a choice, because the tree moved and
             sometimes that is the point.
        """#

    step "1" does="an iteration declares what it continues. Today the only link between two iterations on one slice is that both name the slice, which is a set rather than an order, and nothing can be asked of it"
    step "2" does="a claim a prior iteration on the same slice settled arrives CARRIED, citing that iteration's witness rather than restating it. `pending` is reserved for what has never been shown, so the two stop looking alike"
    step "3" does="an iteration may name the approach it is carrying out. `implement-follows-an-approach` is then satisfied by NAMING the decision, not by having no design — F2's right outcome reached by the right route"
    step "4" does="`the-plan-and-the-code-agree` distinguishes an approach not yet carried out from one abandoned. An in-flight implement is neither followed nor unfollowed, and saying so is different from refusing"
    step "5" does="`a-slice-took-more-than-one-iteration` records WHY, not just that. A slice that took three because the first two were mis-cut and one that took three because the work was large are the same shape and different facts"

    excludes "reopening a closed iteration, or amending its settled claims. Continuation is a \
        NEW iteration standing on a prior one's record; editing the prior one would destroy the \
        trail the continuation exists to stand on"
    excludes "carrying evidence across slices. A claim is unique within its slice, and a witness \
        for one slice's C1 says nothing about another's"
    excludes "deciding when a slice SHOULD take a second iteration. That is the maintainer's \
        judgement and the fitness function's subject; this makes the second one honest, not \
        more or less likely"
    excludes "the three findings' own wording. They are carried against the rules they were \
        filed on and stay there — a finding rewritten to match the fix that closed it stops \
        being evidence that the fix was needed"

    layer "doctrine"    reaches="`pick-up-a-slice` and `close-an-iteration` say what a second iteration inherits and what it still owes; `plan-before-building` says an approach may be carried out by an iteration that did not make it"
    layer "enforcement" reaches="a carried claim is distinguished from a pending one at open and at close; an in-flight approach is not reported as unfollowed; an iteration naming a continuation the record does not hold is refused"
    layer "docs"        reaches="`what does this slice still owe` is answerable across every iteration it has had, instead of only the current one"

    claim "C1" text="an iteration may name the iteration it continues, and one naming a continuation on a different slice — or none the record holds — is refused" \
        settled-by="two negative tests, one naming an iteration on another slice and one naming nothing, each asserting the refusal names what was wrong"
    claim "C2" text="a claim settled by a prior iteration on the same slice arrives carried, citing that iteration's witness" \
        settled-by="a test opening a second iteration on a slice with one settled claim, asserting the claim is carried and the citation names the prior iteration rather than duplicating its digest"
    claim "C3" text="a carried claim may be re-witnessed, and re-witnessing replaces the citation with a run against the tree as it now stands" \
        settled-by="a test attaching evidence to a carried claim and asserting it settles on the new run, since the tree moving is the reason to re-run and the record must be able to say which happened"
    claim "C4" text="an iteration carrying out a prior iteration's approach satisfies `implement-follows-an-approach` by naming it" \
        settled-by="a test over an iteration with no design phase and a named approach, asserting it passes — and one with neither, asserting it does not, which is the case F2 says currently passes"
    claim "C5" text="an approach whose implement phase is in flight is reported as neither followed nor abandoned" \
        settled-by="a test over an iteration with a complete design and an incomplete implement, asserting no refusal — the state F4 says is currently refused, and the only state an iteration spends real time in"
    claim "C6" text="a slice that took more than one iteration records why it did" \
        settled-by="a test asserting a continuation with no stated reason is reported, so the fitness function reads a cause rather than inferring one from a count"

    depends-on "TS.260821.19 — phases have to produce something before an iteration can inherit \
        what a prior one produced"

    useful-alone "the second iteration on any slice stops re-deriving what the record already \
        holds. Every slice this frame has taken more than one pass at has paid this, and the \
        method declares a fitness function that expects more of them, not fewer"

    attacks "S7" "S3"

    useful-to "the-next-agent" "the-team-building-a-digital-product"

    cut-from "three findings raised in one session against three different rules — \
        ITER.260823.08/F1, ITER.260823.08/F2 and ITER.260823.06/F4 — none of which is about the \
        rule it was filed on"

    principle-note "fidelity is computed from the record. A record that can only answer \
        completeness questions about the current iteration computes a fidelity that is true of \
        the iteration and false of the work, and the agent reading it cannot tell which it got"

    proves "a completeness question about a slice is answered from every iteration the slice has had"
    does-not-prove "that a slice taking several iterations is well cut. It makes the second one \
        honest about what the first left; whether there should have been a second is a judgement \
        the record still leaves to the maintainer"
}
